var e=1,t=`pr-factors-01`,n=`portfolio-risk`,r=`Factors: What You Actually Own`,i=`Strategies have names and portfolios have exposures — and the useful question is which underlying risks you are being paid to bear.`,a=`advanced`,o=[`pr-attribution-01`],s=9,c=[`CFA-L2`,`CAIA-L2`,`practical`],l=[{type:`concept`,id:`what-it-is`,body:`A **factor** is a common driver of returns across many assets. The idea is that most of what a portfolio earns comes from exposure to a small number of shared risks rather than from the specific holdings.

The ones with the most evidence behind them:

**Market.** The equity risk premium. The largest factor by some distance.

**Size.** Small companies have historically earned more than large ones, with more risk.

**Value.** Cheap assets on fundamental measures outperforming expensive ones, in long samples and with long painful stretches.

**Momentum.** Recent winners continuing to win, which is the most robust and the least explicable.

**Quality or profitability.** Businesses earning high returns on capital outperforming those that do not.

**Carry.** Earning the yield differential — in currencies, bonds, commodities.

**Volatility.** Selling insurance, in any of its forms.

**Illiquidity.** Compensation for not being able to sell.

**The point of the framework is comparability.** Two strategies with different names, fee structures and vocabularies can be shown to earn the same thing — and once that is visible, the question becomes which is the cheaper way to own it.`},{type:`concept`,id:`strategies-are-factor-bundles`,body:`**Most alternative strategies are bundles of known factors plus a residual, and the residual is what a fee should be for.**

Running a [[regression]] of a strategy's returns against factor returns typically explains a great deal:

**Buyout** — market, size (small), leverage, illiquidity. The residual after those is usually modest.

**[[private-credit|Private credit]]** — credit, carry, illiquidity, short volatility.

**[[long-short-equity|Long/short equity]]** — market beta at some level, often value or momentum, sometimes size.

**[[merger-arbitrage|Merger arbitrage]]** — short volatility plus a small market beta that appears exactly when deals break.

**[[trend-following|Trend following]]** — momentum across asset classes, with a payoff shape unlike the rest.

**Real estate** — rates, credit, and local economic growth.

**This is not a debunking exercise.** Owning factors is how returns are earned, and accessing them through a competent manager is a legitimate decision.

**What it does is change the questions.** Instead of "is this a good manager?", you can ask: which factors am I buying, what am I paying for them, is there a cheaper route to the same exposure, and how much of the return is genuinely unexplained? **The last number is the one worth a performance fee, and it is usually smaller than the fee assumes.**`},{type:`intuition`,id:`double-counting`,body:`**The most common factor problem in an institutional portfolio is not missing a factor. It is owning the same one many times without noticing.**

The mechanism is that allocations are made by strategy name, and factors cut across names. So a portfolio assembled to look diversified by category can be concentrated by exposure:

**Short volatility** appears in merger arbitrage, private credit, covered call overlays, relative value and reinsurance.

**Illiquidity** appears in every private allocation, plus some [[hedge-fund|hedge fund]] strategies holding hard-to-value positions.

**Credit** appears in private credit, distressed, real estate debt, levered loans, and the debt inside every buyout portfolio company.

**Small-cap equity beta** appears in buyout, [[growth-equity|growth equity]], and small-cap public managers.

**A portfolio with fifteen managers across six strategy categories can hold four factors.** And because the factor exposures are shared, the bad scenario for each is the same scenario.

The diagnostic is a factor exposure table rather than an allocation table: **for each holding, list the factors and their approximate weights, then aggregate.** The aggregate is what the portfolio owns.

**It is a dull exercise and it is where the concentration is found**, because the concentration was never visible in a list of strategy names.`},{type:`example`,id:`worked-factors`,body:`An allocator holds five strategies, each 20% of the alternatives sleeve: buyout, private credit, merger arbitrage, real estate, and a covered call overlay.`,walkthrough:[`**Buyout** — equity beta, small-cap tilt, leverage, illiquidity.`,`**Private credit** — credit spread, illiquidity, short volatility, floating rate.`,`**Merger arbitrage** — short volatility, small equity beta on deal breaks.`,`**Real estate** — rates, credit, local growth, illiquidity.`,`**Covered calls** — equity beta, short volatility.`,`Now aggregate. **Short volatility appears in three of five. Illiquidity in three. Credit in two. Equity beta in three.**`,`**Read that carefully: five strategy names, and the portfolio is essentially long equity risk, long credit, short volatility and short liquidity.** Four exposures, and all four deteriorate in the same event.`,`The stress test confirms it: a sharp equity fall with widening spreads and vanishing liquidity damages every one of the five holdings simultaneously.`,`**And a correlation matrix built on calm-period data would have shown this portfolio as diversified**, because in calm periods the idiosyncratic drivers dominate.`,`Sanity check worth reusing: **write each holding's factors in a column and count how many times each factor appears.** Any factor appearing in more than half the portfolio is the portfolio's actual position, whatever the allocation table says.`]},{type:`table`,id:`factor-table`,caption:`Factors, what they pay for, and where they hide`,headers:[`Factor`,`Compensation for`,`Where it appears unlabelled`],rows:[[`Market`,`Bearing undiversifiable equity risk`,`Buyout, growth equity, long/short with [[net-exposure|net exposure]].`],[`Credit`,`Default and downgrade risk`,`Private credit, distressed, real estate debt, buyout portfolio debt.`],[`Short volatility`,`Providing insurance`,`Merger arb, relative value, covered calls, levered credit.`],[`Illiquidity`,`Accepting that you cannot sell`,`Every private allocation, and some hedge funds' hard-to-value books.`],[`[[carry]]`,`Financing, storage or liquidity provision`,`Currency strategies, commodity roll, financing spreads.`],[`Momentum`,`Contested — behavioural or risk-based`,`[[trend-following|Trend following]], and some equity long/short books.`]]},{type:`concept`,id:`how-it-goes-wrong`,body:`**Factor zoo.** Hundreds of published factors, most of which are data-mined and do not survive out of sample. The short list above is what has held up across markets and decades.

**Factor regressions on smoothed returns.** Private strategies' factor loadings are understated for the same reason their volatility is. Lagged factors help; ignoring the problem does not.

**Assuming factor premia are constant.** They vary, sometimes for a decade. Value underperformed for long enough to end careers before recovering.

**Treating factors as free.** Each is compensation for bearing a risk, which means each has periods of loss. A factor that never lost money would not be paid.

**Buying factor exposure twice.** Paying an alternatives fee for exposure available in a cheap systematic vehicle. Worth checking rather than assuming.

**Confusing factor exposure with the strategy's stated intent.** A manager describing themselves as market-neutral with a 0.4 measured beta has an exposure regardless of the description.`},{type:`pitfall`,id:`what-to-pay-for`,body:`**Once a strategy is decomposed into factors, the fee question becomes specific and answerable: what part of this is not available more cheaply?**

The hierarchy is reasonably clear.

**Cheap and widely available:** market beta, and the main equity style factors through systematic vehicles at low cost. Paying an alternatives fee for these is paying for packaging.

**Moderately priced and available:** credit and carry exposures through liquid vehicles, with a liquidity difference that is genuine and should be priced.

**Genuinely requires a manager:** illiquidity, control, access to private companies, and operational improvement. These cannot be bought cheaply because they need someone to do the work.

**Genuinely scarce:** true alpha — return unexplained by any factor. Rare, hard to verify (as the attribution topic showed, decades of data), and the only thing that unambiguously justifies a performance fee.

**So the practical framework: pay index fees for factors, pay real fees for illiquidity and control, and pay performance fees only where the unexplained residual is large and persistent.**

That is not a hostile position toward managers — the middle category is most of the alternatives industry and it earns its fees. **It is a way of knowing which category you are buying**, because the fee schedule rarely says.

And it produces the single most useful diligence question in this whole domain: **what part of your return is not explained by factor exposures, and how do you know?** A manager who has run the regression and can show it is doing something most cannot.`},{type:`onTheJob`,id:`on-the-job`,body:`**Build a factor exposure table across the whole portfolio.** List each holding's factors, aggregate, and count. Any factor in more than half the book is the portfolio's real position.

**Ask each manager what factors explain their returns, and what the residual is.** The answer tells you whether they have looked.

**Check whether any exposure is available in a cheap systematic vehicle.** If it is, the alternatives fee is buying packaging.

**Run factor regressions using lagged factors for private strategies.** Contemporaneous regressions understate the loadings because of the marks.

**Compare a manager's stated exposure with the measured one.** "Market neutral" with a 0.4 beta is an exposure whatever the description says, and it is measurable from returns you already have.`},{type:`keyTakeaways`,id:`takeaways`,items:[`Factors make strategies comparable: two funds with different names and vocabularies can be shown to earn the same thing, and then the question is which is cheaper.`,`Most alternative strategies are factor bundles plus a residual. In the worked portfolio, five strategy names produced four exposures — equity, credit, short volatility and illiquidity.`,`The common failure is owning one factor many times. Short volatility appeared in three of five holdings, and all four exposures deteriorate in the same event.`,`Pay index fees for factors, real fees for illiquidity and control, and performance fees only where the unexplained residual is large and persistent.`]}],u=[{id:`pr-factors-01-q1`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:2,tags:[`factors`,`definition`],concept:`what-it-is`,stem:`What is the main practical value of a factor framework?`,choices:[`It predicts which assets will outperform`,`It makes strategies with different names comparable, by showing which underlying risks each one is being paid to bear`,`It eliminates the need for manager selection`,`It guarantees diversification`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Factors describe compensated risks; they are not forecasts of which specific assets will win.`,`Correct. Once two strategies are expressed in the same exposures, they can be compared on cost, liquidity and residual — which is impossible while they are described in their own vocabularies.`,`It informs manager selection rather than replacing it.`,`It reveals concentration as often as it enables diversification, which is most of its value.`],explanation:`The comparability is the point. It converts "is this a good hedge fund?" into "what exposures does it hold, what do they cost elsewhere, and what is left over?" — three answerable questions in place of one unanswerable one.`},{id:`pr-factors-01-q2`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:3,tags:[`factors`,`concentration`],concept:`double-counting`,stem:`A portfolio holds buyout, private credit, merger arbitrage, real estate and a covered call overlay. Which factor appears most often?`,choices:[`Momentum`,`Short volatility — in private credit, merger arbitrage and the covered call overlay`,`Size`,`Inflation`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`None of the five is primarily a momentum strategy; trend following would be.`,`Correct. Three of the five collect a premium for providing insurance in one form or another, and all three lose in the same conditions.`,`Size appears in buyout and not across the rest.`,`Real estate has some inflation linkage; it is not a shared factor here.`],explanation:`None of these strategies is described as short volatility, which is exactly why the exercise is worth doing. The exposure is in the payoff shape — small regular gains, occasional large losses — rather than in the name.`},{id:`pr-factors-01-q3`,type:`tfj`,difficulty:4,tags:[`factors`,`misconception`],concept:`strategies-are-factor-bundles`,stem:`"Showing that a strategy's returns are mostly explained by factors means the manager adds no value." True or false — and why?`,isTrue:!1,justifications:[`False — accessing factors competently is genuine value, particularly where the exposure requires illiquidity, control or work that cannot be bought cheaply; what changes is what a fair fee looks like`,`False — factor regressions cannot be applied to alternative strategies`,`True — factor-explained returns are by definition not skill`,`False — because factors do not explain returns reliably`],justificationIndex:0,rationales:[`Correct. Someone has to source deals, underwrite credit and improve operations. The decomposition tells you which category of fee is appropriate, not that the manager is redundant.`,`They can be, with care over smoothing and lagged factors.`,`Delivering factor exposure that cannot be bought cheaply is valuable — the illiquidity and control factors require a manager by construction.`,`The short list of established factors explains a great deal of most strategies' returns.`],explanation:`The useful conclusion is a fee hierarchy: index fees for exposures available cheaply, real fees for illiquidity and control, performance fees only for a persistent unexplained residual. It respects what managers do and prices it accurately.`},{id:`pr-factors-01-q4`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:4,tags:[`factors`,`regression`,`on-the-job`],concept:`how-it-goes-wrong`,stem:`Why do factor regressions on private strategy returns understate the true exposures?`,choices:[`Because private strategies genuinely have lower exposures`,`Because the returns are smoothed quarterly estimates, which reduces measured covariance with contemporaneous factor returns`,`Because private strategies report gross of fees`,`Because factor data is unavailable at quarterly frequency`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`A levered buyout portfolio has more equity exposure than the public market, not less.`,`Correct. Partial adjustment each period damps the measured relationship with any contemporaneous series, so the loading appears smaller than the economic exposure.`,`Fee treatment affects the level of returns, not the covariance structure.`,`Factor returns are available at any frequency.`],explanation:`The standard correction is to include lagged factor returns and sum the coefficients, which recovers much of the true loading. It is a five-line change to a regression and it materially alters the conclusion about how much exposure a private allocation carries.`},{id:`pr-factors-01-q5`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:4,tags:[`factors`,`fees`,`on-the-job`],concept:`what-to-pay-for`,stem:`Which exposure most clearly justifies paying a full alternatives fee?`,choices:[`Market beta`,`Illiquidity and control — someone has to source, underwrite and improve the assets, and it cannot be bought in a cheap vehicle`,`Value and momentum style exposure`,`Credit spread exposure to liquid loans`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Available for a few basis points, so paying an alternatives fee for it is paying for packaging.`,`Correct. These require work that cannot be replicated passively, which is why the middle category of the fee hierarchy is where most of the alternatives industry legitimately sits.`,`Available through systematic vehicles at low cost.`,`Available through liquid loan funds and ETFs at a fraction of the fee.`],explanation:`This hierarchy is not hostile to managers — it identifies where fees are earned. It also identifies where they are not, which is the part that rarely appears in a fee negotiation and is entirely computable in advance.`},{id:`pr-factors-01-q6`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:4,tags:[`factors`,`diligence`,`on-the-job`],concept:`what-to-pay-for`,stem:`Which single question best tests whether a manager understands their own return drivers?`,choices:[`What is your target return?`,`What proportion of your returns is explained by factor exposures, and how do you know?`,`How many people are on the investment team?`,`What is your largest position?`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`A target is an intention rather than an analysis of what produces returns.`,`Correct. A manager who has run the regression, knows the loadings and can state the residual has examined their own business. Most have not.`,`Team size describes resources rather than understanding.`,`Position size is a risk detail and does not address the sources of return.`],explanation:`The answer is revealing either way. A manager with the analysis is unusually self-aware and usually easier to underwrite; one who reacts to the question as an accusation has told you they have not looked, which is itself a finding.`}],d=!1,f=null,p={schemaVersion:1,id:t,domain:n,title:r,summary:i,level:a,prereqs:o,estMinutes:9,examRelevance:c,lesson:l,questions:u,needsReview:!1,reviewNote:null};export{p as default,n as domain,s as estMinutes,c as examRelevance,t as id,l as lesson,a as level,d as needsReview,o as prereqs,u as questions,f as reviewNote,e as schemaVersion,i as summary,r as title};
